# The build context is uploaded to the daemon before anything runs. data/ holds
# a 2.5 GB SQLite file plus its WAL; sending that on every build is minutes of
# pure waste, and it must never end up in an image layer anyway — the database
# belongs on a mounted volume.
data/
logs/
scratchpad/
quarantine/
repro/
audits/

**/node_modules/
**/.next/
**/__pycache__/
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
*.bak-*

.git/
.venv/

# Secrets never enter the build context. `COPY daemon/ ./` in the daemon-build
# stage was pulling daemon/.env — SIGNALDECK_API_TOKEN, SIGNALDECK_NVIDIA_KEY,
# SIGNALDECK_TV_WEBHOOK_SECRET — into an intermediate image layer. It does not
# reach the final image (stage 3 copies only the built binaries), but an
# intermediate layer is still a copy of the file on whatever host built it, and
# `docker build --target daemon-build` reaches it directly.
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example

# tools/*.py is copied into the runtime image so the grader can run there (see
# ops/grade.sh). Those scripts are ~1 MB and stdlib-only. tools/alpha/ is the
# research corpus and is the rest of the directory by volume -- excluded so it
# does not ride along in the build context on every build.
tools/alpha/

# freeze/ (8.6 GB) and research/ (2.1 GB) are the frozen corpora and the
# research lab. Nothing in the image comes from either: the Dockerfile COPYs
# only daemon/, web/, tools/*.py, PREREGISTRATION.md and two ops scripts. Left
# in, they made the build context ~10.7 GB, which the daemon sends to the
# builder on EVERY build for nothing.
freeze/
research/
